Subject: x87: Read 80-bit slots directly in PC24 fast bodies, and give one-op runs one.

Requires 0004. Two changes to the single-precision fast body of PC24 runs.

A run's first read of a register still in its 80-bit native slot went
through the generic conversion to binary64 and then the fast body's
narrowing check (FCVT, FCVT, FCMEQ): about 31 instructions per read. The
fast body now builds the f32 from the slot bits when the slot holds a normal
f32 value (integer bit set, the low 40 significand bits clear, exponent in
the normal f32 range) or a signed zero, in about 22 instructions. Every
other encoding (f32 subnormals, values that need more than 24 bits or lie
outside the f32 range, infinities, NaNs, denormals, pseudo-denormals,
unnormals, pseudo-infinities) leaves for the exact body at the same node,
as 0004 already does, so results are unchanged. f32 subnormals and
infinities from a slot, which 0004 kept in the fast body, now take that
exit; they are rare. Registers are taken in the exact read's order, so the
two bodies' allocation state stays identical at every node.

A fast body needed two arithmetic nodes. One is enough: an exact PC24
operation costs about 30 instructions, more than the 7-instruction entry
check plus its single-precision form. Short runs such as fstp m32 of a
slot, fld st(1), fadd m32 now get a fast body.

Measured with ir_pressure_replay on blocks captured from TrackMania Nations
ESWC (executed ARM instructions per block execution, native-slot context,
CW 0x007f): the hottest block, 0x810c12, goes from 482 to 397; 0x42a0e0
953 to 929; 0x945ad0 2908 to 2695; 0x4296b0 is unchanged at 375. Weighted by
execution count, the 17 hottest blocks run 8.4% fewer instructions. The
emitted code of each block, run in an AArch64 emulator with slot values of
every class above, leaves memory and registers identical to 0004's.

tests/test_pc24_slot_reads.c reads every one of those encodings from a slot
in four run shapes, against exact-rational expectations
(generate_pc24_slot_cases.py); it passes under native Rosetta too.

Not done here: the slots a reply leaves in binary64 are converted back to
80 bits by the reply-end export, after both bodies have merged. Writing the
80-bit value directly in the IR epilogue when the run ends the reply would
save about 12 instructions per slot (about 36 on 0x810c12), but changes the
exact body, the slot tracker and the GPR pressure model; left for a later
patch.

